WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:05.259
[delicate music]

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:06.259
Gold.

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:10.170
Born pure and immaculate in the heart of dying
stars, gold has captured the imagination of

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.450
mankind since time immemorial.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:17.240
What enrapturing magic lies in that soft yellow
lustre?

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:23.710
Why has gold enchanted so many for so long
with its soft, pillowy glow and warm, gentle,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.179
yet firm embrace?

00:00:25.179 --> 00:00:30.430
What mysteries lie in gold if we dare to follow
it down the forbidden path into the delicate

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.390
folds of gold’s sweet

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:37.730
Hey, is this getting weird for anyone else?

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:44.040
[aggressive dubstep]

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.199
Gold.

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:57.530
I can’t be the only one who's getting spammed
with ads on the subject of gold, right?

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:02.140
Whether it’s overpolished YouTube spots,
or TikTok finance guys pushing their trading

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:08.860
courses a lot of people seem to want me, Dan
Olson specifically, to buy gold.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.299
Most ads for investments are fairly similar
and just kinda wash into the background radiation

00:01:13.299 --> 00:01:16.800
of daily life, but this one caught my attention.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:26.270
Idris Elba - “I’m going to tell you a
story far more incredible than I ever imagined.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.000
There’s nothing else like it.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.040
A miracle of nature.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.729
I’m talking about gold.”

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:42.090
That is The Suicide Squad’s own Idris Elba
headlining a YouTube documentary on gold distributed

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.770
by the “World Gold Council”... whoever
they are.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.799
I’m assuming they were the villains from
Captain Planet.

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:54.450
‘Gold: A Journey with Idris Elba’ promises
to take the viewer on an eye-pleasing, globe-spanning

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:59.439
adventure peering into the fascinating and
complex relationship between humanity and

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:05.439
element seventy nine, the ways gold intersects
history and technology, and the result is,

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:06.439
well…

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:10.850
I’m not going to bury the lede here, this
is corporate propaganda.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:13.870
It is not particularly good.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.300
But!

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.220
Its existence is interesting.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:23.210
These kinds of celebrity-hosted corporate
spin pieces are a bit of a relic in this day

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:28.910
and age, usually relegated to industry trade
shows or maybe looping on a screen in the

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.330
back corner of some regional museum’s geology
exhibit.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:38.450
The Mineral Journey sponsored by the World
Gold Council.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.160
You know, that kind of thing.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.030
What’s unusual is how hard it’s being
pushed to a lay audience.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:50.709
If you even glanced at something finance related
lately, odds are you got pushed the trailer

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:51.819
as an ad.

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:54.400
“What is i about gold that’s got us so
hooked?

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:04.099
There’s no sticking one in your pocket and
getting out of here is there?”

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:11.870
Now, first of all, in the abstract a documentary
about gold is exactly the kind of thing that

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.010
I’d watch.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:18.280
Gold is a genuinely compelling subject worthy
of exploration, especially by a documentary

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:24.620
team with, like, a travel budget and at least
some access to the operations of a working

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:25.620
mine.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:31.060
You could fill the running time of a documentary
just discussing the chemical properties of

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:37.690
gold, its resistance to corrosion, its malleability,
and how those elemental traits, those quirks

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:42.689
of physics and chemistry, inform its place
in culture.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:48.510
Gold is at once a practical and useful metal,
but also a social construct, an ideoform that

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:52.860
connects its actual uses and various imposed
social meanings.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:57.280
Any comprehensive answer to the question “what
is gold?”

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:04.060
unavoidably carries an emotional component,
and that is a complex thing to unwind.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:08.260
So here’s some fun facts about gold:

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.819
Gold is incredibly resistant to corrosion.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:16.759
The main threats to things humans build and
use are oxygen, water, salt, and uv radiation,

00:04:16.759 --> 00:04:20.900
and gold is, for all intents and purposes,
immune to all of them.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:26.009
It neither rusts nor rots, and aside from
Instagram influencers nothing eats it.

00:04:26.009 --> 00:04:27.440
Gold is pretty rare.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.881
Of the 94 naturally occurring elements found
in Earth’s crust gold is ranked 75th, making

00:04:32.881 --> 00:04:36.540
up 4x10^-7 percent of the crust.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:40.479
Even though it is rare in terms of the total
mass of the earth, its global distribution

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:44.540
is widespread, with significant deposits on
every continent.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:49.100
Also “relatively rare” in the context
of the mass of the earth still means warehouses

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.639
full of the stuff in human terms.

00:04:51.639 --> 00:04:56.280
Despite this rarity gold has historically
been rather convenient, with nearly-pure gold

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.690
nuggets being found in surface-level deposits,
rivers, and former rivers.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:06.440
Since it doesn’t corrode, gold exposed by
the elements stays shiny and easily identifiable.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.480
Gold is extremely malleable, to the point
it can be formed into threads a single-atom

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.910
thick, and then those threads can be stretched
even further before breaking.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:19.750
It can be rolled into sheets so thin that
they become semi-translucent while still retaining

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:20.990
structural integrity.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:27.090
It can be polished so smooth it is the material
of choice when a mirror requires maximum precision,

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.460
such as those in space telescopes.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.530
Gold is hard enough to make useful household
objects out of, but soft enough to be worked

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.840
with stone tools without smelting.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:40.350
Gold isn’t consumed by most of the things
humans use it for and can be readily recycled

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:43.660
and repurposed simply by smelting it into
something new.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:49.430
St. Edward’s Crown could easily be reduced
back to a 2.23 kilo ingot of gold for a second

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:55.220
time in history after English Parliament melted
the original mediaeval crown in 1649.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:56.889
We did it once before, Charles.

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.440
We can do it again.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:03.270
As a result of all of these cosmic coincidences
humans have been using gold since prehistory

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:08.419
and its basic industrialization has been invented
multiple times around the world.

00:06:08.419 --> 00:06:14.160
Putting all that another way, unlike basically
every other workable material at our disposal,

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.570
a trinket made of gold can be sunk to the
bottom of the ocean for tens of thousands

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:23.350
of years and be dug up functionally unchanged.

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:27.970
The timeline of gold is the timeline of geology.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:34.560
This physical trait, this resilience, gives
it an allure of ‘everlasting value’.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:40.740
Even this little cheap little souvenir bottle
of gold wafers comes with a little card promising

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:43.900
it is “a gift of remaining value.”

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:49.080
There is a narrative around gold that starts
in its physical properties but balloons into

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:54.990
its own collection of cultural signifiers,
its own mythology, and any thorough discussion

00:06:54.990 --> 00:07:01.610
about gold will inevitably intersect technology,
colonialism, war, and climate change.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:06.379
So when we talk of gold we’re really talking
about three distinct yet entwined things,

00:07:06.379 --> 00:07:13.090
all sharing a name: gold, the element, gold,
the narrative, and gold, the commodity, and

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:17.530
it is this last one that is the subject of
our conversation today.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:19.870
Not everything is rosy for gold.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:24.090
Gold as a commodity faces a number of challenges
in 2023, some with roots going back decades

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.199
and some more modern.

00:07:26.199 --> 00:07:29.770
Gold has, dare I say, a bit of an image problem.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:34.289
Gold, for a long list of reasons that are
not what this video is about, is a favourite

00:07:34.289 --> 00:07:39.470
of conspiracy theorists, preppers, and and
just, really, a whole host of weirdos.

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:44.330
They hoard it, they accuse governments of
manipulating it, they promote it with an evangelical

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.289
zeal.

00:07:46.289 --> 00:07:50.190
“Come in.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.800
How do you plan on supporting my granddaughter
young man?

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.730
I’m only 17, sir.

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:57.009
What can provide her with the brightest future?

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.060
A home?

00:07:58.060 --> 00:07:59.060
Wrong!

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:00.060
Gold!

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.060
Gold?

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:05.639
It’s never too early or late to secure your
future with gold!”

00:08:05.639 --> 00:08:07.360
For my younger viewers, this is real.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.653
This isn’t, this isn’t a Tim and Eric
sketch or a bit from The Daily Show, that’s

00:08:11.653 --> 00:08:16.190
the guy who did burglaries for Nixon sincerely
shilling gold.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:21.129
So safe to say nothing attracts a crank quite
like gold and that definitely impacts how

00:08:21.129 --> 00:08:24.250
the wider public thinks of gold as an investment.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:28.500
“Call Rosland Capital and tell them Gordon
Liddy sent you.”

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.750
Alright, I want to take a second just to unwind
the facts of this.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:37.599
Despite the popularity of gold amongst people
who own doomsday bunkers, the rationale that

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:40.289
they’re working from isn’t un-sound.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:45.850
If you were an investor back in the Netherlands
in 1635, which asset would deliver a better

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:50.529
return over almost 400 years, tulips or gold?

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:56.430
In the long run you can be pretty confident
that any gold you have will still be abstractly

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:59.170
valuable in the indeterminate future.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:05.019
So on one hand gold as an abstract financial
instrument is unlikely to go anywhere, but

00:09:05.019 --> 00:09:11.339
on the other hand while gold is a safe bet
for your 400-year plan, it’s less of a sure

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:14.019
thing in your 5-year portfolio.

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:18.070
Gold doesn’t pay interest, so it didn’t
thrive in the low-inflation, low-interest

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:23.710
climate of the bull run, but it’s also less
appealing in our current high-interest climate.

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:29.170
And for the “sound money” gold-standard
Austrian Economics crowd, gold has seen heavy

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:31.570
competition from cryptocurrency.

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:36.750
The majority of the accessible gold has probably
already been mined, and after hundreds of

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:40.710
years of panning and digging we’ve found
most of the easy stuff.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:45.640
New discoveries and new operations are becoming
increasingly expensive.

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:51.260
As the documentary demonstrates with breathless
wonder, many modern mines, particularly the

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:57.000
big, high-production mines, are thousands
of metres deep, and all mines involve displacing

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.079
massive volumes of rock.

00:09:59.079 --> 00:10:01.089
“You feel stress?”

00:10:01.089 --> 00:10:08.610
“It’s that, it’s the idea that we're
going down into the, into the centre of the

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.680
earth.”

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:13.040
Mines are also under increasing scrutiny.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:17.670
The average consumer is more aware than ever
of the numerous impacts of gold mining.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:22.870
There’s both consciousness of the systemic
impacts, as well as greater access to information

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:24.100
about working conditions.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:29.200
For instance, Less than two weeks before the
documentary was released, a gold mine in Zimbabwe

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.120
collapsed, killing at least nine miners.

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:37.209
So, we’re going to, for now, set aside the
question of if these mines should exist at

00:10:37.209 --> 00:10:41.540
all, whether or not the gold that they’re
digging up even needs to be dug up in the

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:47.550
first place, and just for a moment put ourselves
in the mindset of a mine operator.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:52.800
We don’t need to empathise with them, but
we do need to understand their incentives.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.490
Mine operators don’t dig up and hoard gold
like they’re playing Minecraft, they dig

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.910
it up and sell it.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:04.269
Making a profit from a gold mine requires
the company to thread a needle.

00:11:04.269 --> 00:11:09.959
Mining operations have enormous upfront costs,
and demand for gold is driven overwhelmingly

00:11:09.959 --> 00:11:13.390
by bearish sentiment elsewhere.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:16.899
Gold has its best days when the market is
crashing.

00:11:16.899 --> 00:11:24.089
This means that operating a gold mine can
be precarious, and the mine is extremely exposed

00:11:24.089 --> 00:11:26.149
to the price of gold.

00:11:26.149 --> 00:11:31.850
Even small increases in the value of gold
translates to massive profits for the mines.

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:37.550
And in reverse, small decreases or lengthy
lulls, can be dire.

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:43.060
Gold may be the safe pick in the long term,
but in the short term, investment in gold

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:45.779
can lead to a lot of anxiety.

00:11:45.779 --> 00:11:48.149
Enter the World Gold Council.

00:11:48.149 --> 00:11:53.260
The WGC is one of the world’s leading authorities
on gold; and you’d hope so, given that the

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:57.390
Council is comprised exclusively of major
gold mining companies.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:01.770
The organisation’s sole purpose is to push
demand for gold.

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:05.480
That isn’t an accusation, it’s not even
a provocative statement.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:10.389
The World Gold Council is extremely up front
with its objective to promote the interests

00:12:10.389 --> 00:12:14.420
of its members, who are, again, big mining
companies.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:20.360
The organisation was founded in mid-1987 by
the most “forward-thinking mining companies”.

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:25.510
And they were absolutely forward-thinking,
because before the end of the year gold’s

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:31.790
value would begin a sustained decline that
would persist for the next 15 years.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:35.589
These sorts of lobby and marketing groups
are actually pretty common, promoting not

00:12:35.589 --> 00:12:41.890
just a specific company or brand but an entire
class of product, and they’re all technically

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:44.740
non-profit organizations as a result.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:49.310
The World Gold Council exists in a weird spot
on the spectrum of propaganda.

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:54.660
They lobby governments, they spin market trends,
and they greenwash mining activities, but

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:59.912
their ultimate goal is to convince serious
investors to buy gold directly and to put

00:12:59.912 --> 00:13:02.149
their money into gold mines.

00:13:02.149 --> 00:13:09.130
Their clients sell literal gold, the WGC sells
the idea of gold.

00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:16.470
“You are precious, you are brilliance, you
are bold, you are gold”

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:20.579
“Bro, I’ve been there, but gold saved
my portfolio”

00:13:20.579 --> 00:13:23.959
“A tip for you.

00:13:23.959 --> 00:13:24.959
Visit invest dot gold.”

00:13:24.959 --> 00:13:28.670
“Visit invest dot gold to see why gold is
everyone’s asset”

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:31.410
“Stability, security, protection.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:35.980
You shouldn’t have to choose.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:36.980
Gold.

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:39.240
Your strategic advantage.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.510
Visit goldhub dot com.”

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:47.779
Like, this isn’t just online crank forum
stuff, these aren’t meme stocks, they don’t

00:13:47.779 --> 00:13:52.649
represent tiny “artisanal” mines that
consist of three dudes sharing the one jackhammer.

00:13:52.649 --> 00:13:58.170
Their main goal is convincing investors to
give millions of dollars to industrial operations

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:04.529
that will move millions of tons of rock; they
can’t have a reputation for spreading disinformation.

00:14:04.529 --> 00:14:09.700
So while their job is propaganda, they are
considered a reputable source for research

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:11.160
on gold.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:16.230
But that doesn’t stop the World Gold Council
from being implicated in countless conspiracy

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:19.670
theories, typically painting it as a psy-op.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:24.740
“I long concluded that the World Gold Council
exists to make sure there never is a World

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:25.930
Gold Council.”

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:29.529
So you can try, but you’ll never escape
the cranks.

00:14:29.529 --> 00:14:37.389
“Well there’s some of us who have suspected
since GLD was put together that it is really

00:14:37.389 --> 00:14:43.139
a, it’s another mechanism in the tool of
the central bankers and their bullion banks

00:14:43.139 --> 00:14:50.759
to be able to use the public’s investment
gold to apply it to hot spots in the market

00:14:50.759 --> 00:14:55.620
to keep the price down, that it’s another
tool of gold price suppression.”

00:14:55.620 --> 00:15:00.720
So, “Gold: A Journey with Idris Elba”
is gold mining propaganda.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:05.250
Again, don’t take it from us, take it from
Joe Cavatoni, chief marketing strategist for

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:06.810
the World Gold Council.

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:12.091
“The documentary was commissioned, simply
put, to support our ambition as an organisation

00:15:12.091 --> 00:15:17.181
to help the world really understand the value
of gold, and really the experience of its

00:15:17.181 --> 00:15:18.890
transformative power, you know.”

00:15:18.890 --> 00:15:21.970
We’re going to come back to this interview
a few times.

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:27.690
It’s… very interesting, but, yeah, then
he just lists some mining industry talking

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:28.690
points.

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:31.579
“Yeah I think there’s a couple of key
messages that are in the film.

00:15:31.579 --> 00:15:33.560
I’m just gonna highlight a few.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:38.350
The first is that, for every one job that
a gold mine produces six indirect jobs, or

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:41.589
jobs around the community of that gold mine
get created.

00:15:41.589 --> 00:15:43.139
A lot of people don’t realise that.”

00:15:43.139 --> 00:15:47.520
It is as subtle as, a gold-plated toilet

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:51.980
The documentary itself has a bit more of a
deft hand when it comes to smuggling the agenda

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:56.100
in with the info but, not that deft a hand.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:16:00.319
“These things, even though they mean a lot
to the artist, but they actually carry the

00:16:00.319 --> 00:16:02.350
weight of the money that they”

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:03.350
“100%”

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:05.839
“Like you know it’s not like it’s ever
gonna depreciate.”

00:16:05.839 --> 00:16:06.839
“100%.

00:16:06.839 --> 00:16:08.100
It’s the same thing.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:09.899
Buying gold, they’re investments.”

00:16:09.899 --> 00:16:11.230
That isn’t subtext, that’s just text.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:16.100
The documentary allegedly features Idris Elba
supposedly going on a globe-trotting adventure

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:19.649
to shed light on humanity’s ancient relationship
to gold.

00:16:19.649 --> 00:16:24.231
In actuality, it’s a virtual-tour of gold
mines that all belong to members of the World

00:16:24.231 --> 00:16:29.560
Gold Council, and each segment exists to either
counter a popular criticism of the gold mining

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:33.569
industry, or pitch the mine as an investment
opportunity.

00:16:33.569 --> 00:16:38.870
Elba visits mines in Canada and South Africa,
meets the gold-bedecked Ashanti King, Otumfour

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:44.009
Osei Tutu II in Ghana, gets a tour of a Bank
of England gold vault, hangs out with famous

00:16:44.009 --> 00:16:48.910
hip-hop jeweller Abs the Jeweller, and narrates
segments on mine clean up, gold jewellery

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:54.230
in India, the California gold rush, and a
straight up marketing pitch for gold prospecting

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:55.230
in Peru.

00:16:55.230 --> 00:17:01.529
Like, just to be clear - every mine credited
in the documentary belongs to a WGC member

00:17:01.529 --> 00:17:02.529
corporation.

00:17:02.529 --> 00:17:04.040
And they play us all the hits.

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:08.790
Gold mines foster local economies, they build
infrastructure, mining companies totally clean

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:12.890
up after themselves, they’re even on the
front lines of battling malaria.

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:13.890
Though…

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:19.730
On that note… even the doc doesn’t bother
to pretend that battling malaria is an altruistic

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:20.730
endeavour.

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:25.329
It’s because they lose more to sick days
than the cost of R&amp;D on new pesticides.

00:17:25.329 --> 00:17:31.120
“Out of the work force of eight thousand
employees, every month you had about six thousand

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:35.530
eight hundred of these employees and their
dependants report into the mine hospital for

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:36.530
malaria.

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:41.160
So the mine decided to establish a malaria
control program.”

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:45.330
It’s hard to overlook the fact that the
documentary’s global scope has less to do

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:50.679
with the grandness of the subject matter,
and more a matter of cherry-picking.

00:17:50.679 --> 00:17:56.220
The documentary is unique partly because it
would be utterly banal in a different context.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:18:01.090
The film is composed of a series of four to
six minute sequences that, again, would normally

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:04.549
be looping in the corner of a museum installation.

00:18:04.549 --> 00:18:10.350
The fact that something so insubstantial is
being pushed so hard is kinda fascinating

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:11.350
in itself.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:15.240
They had Idris-Elba money, and chose to spend
it on this.

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:18.510
“Wow, look at how hot that is.”

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:22.830
On that note, a common discussion point about
the doc has been Idris Elba’s performance.

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:24.520
It’s very strange.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:26.580
There are times when Idris is energised.

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:31.510
“So today is the very special Ashanti Akwasidae
Festival.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:37.480
I’ve been honoured to be asked to be part
of the procession, and it is an honour.”

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:41.340
There are times when he’s clearly working
hard to sell this thing.

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:43.539
“into the… centre of the Earth.”]

00:18:43.539 --> 00:18:44.539
“I tell you what, it’s so exciting.

00:18:44.539 --> 00:18:45.539
This is so exciting.

00:18:45.539 --> 00:18:50.490
It’s the first time I’ve ever seen gold
being poured.”

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:54.120
And in the sound booth, Idris has a very stilted
energy.

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:58.710
It could be a deliberate choice, but it feels
kind he’s cold reading the script off a

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:01.650
teleprompting that is slightly too far away.

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:05.430
“But for me, and I’m pretty sure I’m
not alone here, the best thing about gold

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:08.140
is being able to wear it.”

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:12.110
For comparison, here is Elba’s performance
in a different doc.

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:17.160
“To understand how club culture has become
so engrained in our modern lives, we need

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:20.570
to go back to a scene that started on New
York’s underground.

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:22.970
It was the 70s, and the city was suffering.”

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:27.930
Idris has close ties to Ghana and has spent
several years working to develop a film studio

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:29.120
in the region.

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:33.430
During his trip where he met the Ashanti King,
he also met the President of Ghana to discuss

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:38.541
policy - and was planning to bring an upcoming
film of his to the region.

00:19:38.541 --> 00:19:43.730
"...we could case study my film as a proof
of concept…"

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:48.169
To what extent this influenced his decision
to do the project, we can only speculate.

00:19:48.169 --> 00:19:53.670
But it’s plainly obvious that Idris really
turns up in Ghana, and less so in Quebec.

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:54.670
“Wow.

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:56.930
Look at how hot that is.”

00:19:56.930 --> 00:20:00.909
What we’re left with is a very standard
piece of corporate communication with a celebrity

00:20:00.909 --> 00:20:04.100
voice actor and overly dramatic framing.

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:07.730
For the most part, the documentary is just
boring.

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:11.650
The doc makes boring decisions for self-interested
reasons.

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:16.179
By way of example, India has the second highest
demand for gold after China.

00:20:16.179 --> 00:20:20.270
The two combine to make up half the world’s
demand for gold.

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:25.030
This makes the World Gold Council very invested
in the Indian gold market.

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:31.390
So we get a short sequence in India that alludes
to the cultural significance of gold in India.

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:36.780
This is a big and genuinely interesting subject,
but as will be a common theme, the doc doesn’t

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:39.770
have time to do ‘interesting’.

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:44.340
The nuanced significance of gold in Hindu
and Ashanti cultures are flanderised into

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.490
this very odd and uniform notion of “respect”.

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:51.750
Here’s Joe specifically responding to a
question about the importance of treating

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:56.059
gold with “spiritual respect”... whatever
that means.

00:20:56.059 --> 00:21:00.260
“We come on your program regularly, we talk
about gold demand trends, we talk about the

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:04.490
significance of India as a market.

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:09.630
Play close attention where Idris sees a young
couple getting married.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:13.370
They talk in great detail about how its significant
beyond the dowry, the wealth creation, or

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:23.049
the passing on of that dowry, it's more about
how it fits into the society…”

00:21:23.049 --> 00:21:24.990
Okay, so, this doesn’t actually happen.

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:29.809
For one; Idris isn’t there, he doesn’t
see anything, but besides that the documentary

00:21:29.809 --> 00:21:32.799
only focuses on the bride’s gold jewellery.

00:21:32.799 --> 00:21:37.960
The dowry isn’t acknowledged at all, likely
because it’s a complicated, thorny, topic

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:40.049
to throw at a western audience.

00:21:40.049 --> 00:21:45.770
But this is the essence of the documentary
on the whole: by volume, it is mostly tacky

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:50.809
but inoffensive corporate spin on a shallow
engagement with the subject matter.

00:21:50.809 --> 00:21:55.659
But there are, of course, issues that are
too big even for propaganda to just omit.

00:21:55.659 --> 00:22:00.750
The subjects of colonialism and environment
being the two major ones.

00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:05.029
The doc has a sequence in Johannesburg where
the subjects of race and working conditions

00:22:05.029 --> 00:22:07.850
are quarantined.

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:16.480
The film cedes some ground on this, acknowledging
that historically gold mining maybe… briefly…

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:17.820
intersected with racism.

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:22.230
Elba interviews Frans Baleni, who worked in
apartheid-era gold mines and was a leader

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.430
of a key miner’s strike.

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:27.050
During that interview, the two don’t pull
any punches.

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:29.930
They are carrying no water for the mines.

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:31.790
“we were not really looked after.

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:38.100
We were almost like slaves, because what they
needed is your labour, nothing else.”

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:41.610
However, this film was commissioned by mining
companies.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:42.760
So…

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:45.620
“There’s no hiding from the fact

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:46.750
No hiding, yeah

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:49.230
“that gold mining played a major part”

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:50.230
Uh huh

00:22:50.230 --> 00:22:53.270
“in shaping South African society during
the apartheid years.”

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:54.270
Yeah?!

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:56.130
“But with gold”

00:22:56.130 --> 00:22:58.299
[Airhorn noises]

00:22:58.299 --> 00:23:03.770
The doc then very awkwardly frames gold as
a liberating force for black miners.

00:23:03.770 --> 00:23:09.970
“But with gold comes power, and those gold
miners, they knew that.”

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:15.750
Because you see… gold, by being so useful,
gave the miners a bargaining chip.

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:19.930
By withholding their labour, and denying the
country access to the gold… really, the

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:21.779
black community was empowered.

00:23:21.779 --> 00:23:28.210
It’s a subtle reframing of a labour strike
not as the withholding of labour, but as the

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:30.290
withholding of product.

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:35.000
An inert mineral is stealing the valour of
labour unions.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:36.669
It’s very weird.

00:23:36.669 --> 00:23:40.809
We’re then abruptly moved to South Deep,
an active mine in the region that is making

00:23:40.809 --> 00:23:43.880
use of remote vehicles operated from the surface.

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:48.549
This is an interesting novelty, and genuinely
represents an improvement in working conditions

00:23:48.549 --> 00:23:53.169
for at least some stuff, but it doesn’t
really seem to follow the heavy discussion

00:23:53.169 --> 00:23:54.730
of Apartheid.

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:58.700
But as they talk about mechanization, they
talk about how it has done at least something

00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:05.159
for gender equality amongst the workers, and
then this segment’s purpose comes into focus.

00:24:05.159 --> 00:24:10.400
“There are ladies who are miners, crew leaders,
and then production supervisors”

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:13.929
Its purpose in the Text is to absolve the
industry.

00:24:13.929 --> 00:24:18.890
By saying ‘here is what happened then, and
here is what is happening now’, the text

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:22.360
argues that mining companies have fixed the
problem.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:23.600
“Chu chu chu”

00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:28.760
That’s already wild on its face, but even
within the doc it’s absurd.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:34.450
One is a serious, politically charged conversation
dealing with the subject of race and systemic

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:40.320
exploitation; the other is about the quirks
of driving a $5 million RC truck for a day

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:41.320
job.

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:45.830
The argument does not land at all, but the
documentary just moves on with an entirely

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:51.240
undeserved confidence that it has tackled
the subject of race in the global mining sector.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:54.210
Then there’s the subject of the environment.

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:58.149
Obviously there is no nuanced discussion of
the environmental impacts of mining where

00:24:58.149 --> 00:25:01.059
the companies come out of it looking good.

00:25:01.059 --> 00:25:07.140
So the mandate is simple: say as little as
possible to omit issues, while playing up

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:08.140
successes.

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:11.570
So, that explains why the New Zealand segment
is the way it is.

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:12.779
But it just sucks.

00:25:12.779 --> 00:25:14.200
It sucks so bad.

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:19.020
The topic is specifically on the closure and
restoration of sites; mining companies ‘tidying

00:25:19.020 --> 00:25:21.140
up’ after themselves.

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:25.419
The segment focuses on the reclamation of
Reefton Mine, located on the south island

00:25:25.419 --> 00:25:26.480
of New Zealand.

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:31.169
The entire sequence is four minutes long,
of which there’s maybe 90 seconds of footage

00:25:31.169 --> 00:25:32.270
of the mine.

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:36.010
We see that the main pit has been filled with
water, and we’re shown the process used

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:38.179
to plant seeds from a helicopter.

00:25:38.179 --> 00:25:43.120
We’re then treated to a CG render of what
the site is expected to look like at some

00:25:43.120 --> 00:25:45.299
unstated point in the future.

00:25:45.299 --> 00:25:49.470
We close on some B-roll of wildlife, and we’re
done with New Zealand.

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:53.029
Here is the totality of material on the pit.

00:25:53.029 --> 00:26:00.200
“When the mine closed in 2016, their first
task was to fill the main pit, to turn it

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:02.770
into a lake.

00:26:02.770 --> 00:26:10.470
It’s nice, but it still looks like a mine
to me”

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:14.220
If I held stock in Oceana Gold I would be
furious.

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:18.390
Anyone with any general knowledge of mining
is going to come away from this doc certain

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:23.620
that this is a pit lake - the same toxic,
corrosive hellholes that you find in a Tom

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:24.620
Scott video.

00:26:24.620 --> 00:26:29.640
“It is a mile wide and so deep you could
fit the new One World Trade Center standing

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:31.309
upright in it.

00:26:31.309 --> 00:26:35.840
Or at least you could if the pit wasn’t
halfway filled with toxic, dilute acid.”

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:39.110
“It looks inviting, but you wouldn’t want
to swim in there.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:41.120
That is dark, lonely water.

00:26:41.120 --> 00:26:43.320
It looks toxically blue, and it is.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:46.700
It’s alkaline, it’s got a pH above 11,
it would be like swimming in dilute bleach.”

00:26:46.700 --> 00:26:50.020
“Tell you what that water’s nice and blue
to me.”

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:56.570
But the Reefton pit is not like those pit
lakes, and this site is notable for precisely

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:59.909
that reason - that’s why we’re here.

00:26:59.909 --> 00:27:02.179
Okay, just to back up for a sec.

00:27:02.179 --> 00:27:03.929
We all know about abandoned mines.

00:27:03.929 --> 00:27:08.430
A company would work a mine until either the
mine or the company went bust, and then they

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:10.510
would just walk away.

00:27:10.510 --> 00:27:15.430
In the case of open-cut mines like Reefton,
the pits often fill with rainwater over time

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:17.900
to create artificial bodies of water.

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:23.309
Pit lakes, or quarry lakes, are exceptionally
dangerous places for a whole host of reasons

00:27:23.309 --> 00:27:25.990
that we’ll just put up on screen here for
a moment.

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:31.180
The majority of fatalities at abandoned mines
are attributed to drowning in pit lakes.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:35.610
You may also be familiar with famous sites
like the Berkeley Pit in Butte, Montanna.

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:41.059
Berkeley’s pit lake is so contaminated by
copper that for about a decade copper was

00:27:41.059 --> 00:27:43.929
“mined” directly from the lake water.

00:27:43.929 --> 00:27:48.320
This makes the water toxic, and if the pit
lake fills beyond a certain point, the forbidden

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:54.460
gatorade will spill into the general water
supply, which is considered generally undesirable.

00:27:54.460 --> 00:27:59.740
In response the US Environmental Protection
Agency has mandated that the lake must never

00:27:59.740 --> 00:28:05.890
reach that critical level, so the water must
be actively pumped out in perpetuity, but

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:11.860
even with that over the years they’ve had
to just keep making the thing bigger because

00:28:11.860 --> 00:28:17.360
it’s really hard to keep water from getting
into an existing lake.

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:22.919
The Berkeley Pit is a particularly high profile
example, but there are thousands of pit lakes

00:28:22.919 --> 00:28:25.990
out there, each with their own risk factors.

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:29.320
Believe it or not Tom Scott hasn’t even
visited half of them!

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:35.750
This is not a minor problem, this is one of
the fundamental byproducts of the process:

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:41.250
the thing you are doing at an elemental level
is digging a huge hole.

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:48.120
Until very recently it was considered progressive
to enforce what is called a ‘lock and leave’

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:49.120
policy.

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:53.740
Mining companies can leave behind a pit that
will fill with toxic water, but they at least

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:55.919
need to put up a fence.

00:28:55.919 --> 00:29:00.100
Needless to say this doesn’t do much to
mitigate the harm of these pits.

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:04.990
Once a pit lake is established, it’s incredibly
difficult to unwind, because now you’re

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:09.929
dealing with massive volumes of water, which
is both very heavy in its own right and does

00:29:09.929 --> 00:29:14.340
not much care for where you do and do not
want it to go.

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:19.070
So the solution is simple, don’t let mining
companies leave sites in a state that will

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:21.200
produce a toxic pit.

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:24.769
That is a complex, multi-disciplinary process.

00:29:24.769 --> 00:29:27.480
So, that brings us back to Reefton.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:32.169
In 2004, OceanaGold received approval to operate
the Reefton Mine.

00:29:32.169 --> 00:29:36.480
As part of this, they were subject to heavy
obligations to restore the site upon closure

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:42.039
of the mine- which included the posting of
a performance bond that, like a rental bond,

00:29:42.039 --> 00:29:47.429
was money set aside to cover the restoration
of the land in the event Oceana went bust.

00:29:47.429 --> 00:29:51.760
An operations director for the Department
of Conservation praised the company’s work,

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:54.600
describing it as “world class”.

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:59.640
Unlike mining operators in the past, OceanaGold
prepared for the closing of the mine years

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:00.710
ahead of time.

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:04.620
They’re one of the first to make use of
a new water treatment system conceptualised

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:05.900
at Cardiff University.

00:30:05.900 --> 00:30:11.230
“By oxygenating the water before it enters
the Vertical Flow Reactor the iron within

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:15.640
the water comes out of solution, turning it
a reddish-brown colour.

00:30:15.640 --> 00:30:20.600
The iron particulate then gently settles on
a gravel bed at the bottom of the ponds.

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:25.480
Iron naturally attracts other metals, and
so is able to capture free-floating arsenic

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:26.570
from the water.

00:30:26.570 --> 00:30:31.539
The water continues its gravity fed course
through the gravel bed and exits the system

00:30:31.539 --> 00:30:33.090
into Devils Creek.

00:30:33.090 --> 00:30:38.630
Extensive testing has proven that the Vertical
Flow Reactor reliably removes excess arsenic

00:30:38.630 --> 00:30:40.210
and iron from the water.

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:45.649
Over time, the metals will be exhausted from
the leachable area of the surrounding rock

00:30:45.649 --> 00:30:48.990
and the pods will continue to naturally spill
into Devils Creek”

00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:53.330
I’m sorry, why wasn’t any of this in the
documentary?

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:56.930
The helicopter is not what makes Reefton interesting.

00:30:56.930 --> 00:31:02.039
It’s the ecological restoration that, literally,
gets into the weeds.

00:31:02.039 --> 00:31:06.200
“The wooding material that we’ve placed
around the restoration sites also provide

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:10.690
little micro-climates for other species that
we wouldn’t have planted ourselves, such

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:12.120
as these ferns.”

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:15.570
Reefton may be the best example of reclamation
on the planet.

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:19.820
The World Gold Council must think so, otherwise
we wouldn’t be here.

00:31:19.820 --> 00:31:24.680
But the doc doesn’t want to dig into the
subject of restoration in any detail because

00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:31.409
to celebrate what makes Reefton noteworthy
would require admitting that it is exceptional.

00:31:31.409 --> 00:31:35.730
‘Hey, these guys have almost cracked the
toxic pit problem.’

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:37.990
‘Wait, what toxic pit problem?’

00:31:37.990 --> 00:31:42.600
It’s also hard to overlook the fact that
vertical flow reactors are being implemented

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:45.730
in New Zealand, South Korea, and the USA.

00:31:45.730 --> 00:31:47.530
You know, wealthy nations.

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:52.139
It’s one thing for New Zealand to strong-arm
OceanaGold into restoring a site adjacent

00:31:52.139 --> 00:31:56.600
to a national park, but what about countries
in the global south?

00:31:56.600 --> 00:32:00.980
Human rights organizations have repeatedly
found OceanaGold in violation of indigenous

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:07.260
rights around their Didipio mine dating back
to 2007, accusations including illegal evictions

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:12.600
and arson, and have repeatedly recommended
that their licence to mine be revoked.

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:13.880
It was not.

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:18.649
And the UN has denounced the “unnecessary
and disproportionate” use of force against

00:32:18.649 --> 00:32:22.210
local residents as recently as 2020.

00:32:22.210 --> 00:32:27.110
Can Filipino residents expect OceanaGold to
implement world class restoration techniques

00:32:27.110 --> 00:32:29.679
to restore their land?

00:32:29.679 --> 00:32:31.020
Odds aren’t great!

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:36.480
The work being done at Reefton is interesting
and important - it is exceptional in every

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:37.929
sense of the word.

00:32:37.929 --> 00:32:42.799
The documentary went to New Zealand to cherry
pick the most radical project it could find,

00:32:42.799 --> 00:32:44.740
but still managed to stuff it up.

00:32:44.740 --> 00:32:49.370
The documentary is only willing to engage
with the superficial elements of ecological

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:52.600
restoration - specifically the tree planting.

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:55.299
“It’s really just gardening.

00:32:55.299 --> 00:32:57.159
But supersized.”

00:32:57.159 --> 00:33:03.399
In its brief runtime, the documentary focuses
exclusively on aesthetics, which is explicitly

00:33:03.399 --> 00:33:06.130
not what the restoration project is about.

00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:10.230
“So we’re not trying to hide the fact
that we mined here, but we’re just trying

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:12.490
our best to put it back.”

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:17.450
The community doesn’t need the mine’s
existence obscured - they need the company

00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:23.730
to clean up their crap and avoid poisoning
the groundwater; which is what they’re doing.

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:28.309
But the documentary would leave viewers believing
that the project is greenwashing - simply

00:33:28.309 --> 00:33:31.390
planting trees around an inevitable hazard.

00:33:31.390 --> 00:33:35.660
The documentary manages to misrepresent both
angles of the subject.

00:33:35.660 --> 00:33:41.169
It omits the actual reason why closed mines
are so dangerous, and the meaningful ecological

00:33:41.169 --> 00:33:44.049
restoration being carried out at the site.

00:33:44.049 --> 00:33:48.350
The result is that the environmental activists
are pissed off, the company’s shareholders

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:51.920
are pissed off, and you, the viewer, are bored.

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:53.159
This sucks.

00:33:53.159 --> 00:33:57.269
Once the doc is satisfied that it has addressed
all your concerns, and made its case for gold

00:33:57.269 --> 00:34:01.519
as a thing, it’s happy to drop the pretense
and fixate on the financials for the last

00:34:01.519 --> 00:34:02.710
15 minutes.

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:05.130
Idris is given a tour of the Bank of England.

00:34:05.130 --> 00:34:10.350
“I’ve got an appointment with the bank’s
chief cashier.

00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:14.720
One of the very few people with access to
its vaults.

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:16.760
Sarah John.”

00:34:16.760 --> 00:34:23.129
Anyway, Idris gawks at gold bullion for a
bit, before taking a very brief aside to tastelessly

00:34:23.129 --> 00:34:25.349
discuss the 2004 Boxing Day tsunami.

00:34:25.349 --> 00:34:31.230
“On the 26th of December, 2004, there was
a massive tsunami in the Indian Ocean.”

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:37.860
The Boxing Day tsunami is the deadliest natural
disaster of the 21st century, with over 130,000

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:42.859
confirmed deaths in Indonesia alone, mostly
concentrated in the province of Aceh.

00:34:42.859 --> 00:34:48.820
“But in Aceh, there was something unusual
about the community that helped it recover.

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:53.990
You see, most people didn’t have saving
accounts.

00:34:53.990 --> 00:35:02.490
Instead, they had gold ingots and jewellery,
which meant they could quickly use it as collateral

00:35:02.490 --> 00:35:07.940
and start putting their lives back together.”

00:35:07.940 --> 00:35:12.990
So we get that little gold nugget, before
immediately moving on to hang out with a celebrity

00:35:12.990 --> 00:35:13.990
jeweller.

00:35:13.990 --> 00:35:19.980
“Abtin Abbasi, or Abs for short, is part
of a new wave in jewelry that began with Hip

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:20.980
Hop.

00:35:20.980 --> 00:35:22.900
“Like you know it’s not like it’s ever
gonna depreciate.” “100%.

00:35:22.900 --> 00:35:24.170
It’s the same thing.

00:35:24.170 --> 00:35:25.440
Buying gold, they’re investments.”

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:29.580
The final segment is functionally an ad for
Newmont’s operations in Peru, which are

00:35:29.580 --> 00:35:33.570
still in their speculative stages before the
doc wraps up.

00:35:33.570 --> 00:35:35.580
“So, the story of gold.

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:38.990
To me, it’s really a love story.

00:35:38.990 --> 00:35:42.900
Like all romances, it’s had its up and downs.”

00:35:42.900 --> 00:35:44.910
What downs Idris?

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:45.910
What downs?

00:35:45.910 --> 00:35:47.319
What do you think the downs might be?

00:35:47.319 --> 00:35:52.020
Here, here, I’ve got my notes ready to go,
I can help make a list, what are the downs?

00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:59.560
“They’re all part of our relationship
with this beautiful seductive metal”

00:35:59.560 --> 00:36:03.630
Hey, is this getting weird for anyone else?

00:36:03.630 --> 00:36:07.369
The core audience for the documentary has
received it one of two ways.

00:36:07.369 --> 00:36:10.020
Your standard Goldbugs have lovebombed the
doc.

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:14.450
Like crypto bros and Apes, there is a vested
interest in the documentary’s success as

00:36:14.450 --> 00:36:19.560
a tool of propaganda, leading to the same
types of astroturf techniques.

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:23.560
On the complete other end of the spectrum,
the crackpot breed of goldbug has responded

00:36:23.560 --> 00:36:25.990
to the film with outright hostility.

00:36:25.990 --> 00:36:31.970
Framing it as a deliberate misinformation
to distract from the truth about gold… whatever

00:36:31.970 --> 00:36:32.970
that may be.

00:36:32.970 --> 00:36:37.080
“I’m willing to bet your coming WGC gold
documentary will be more like a childlike

00:36:37.080 --> 00:36:38.080
cartoon.

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:41.630
You won’t dare to try to choke down some
of these ongoing spicy facts regarding the

00:36:41.630 --> 00:36:45.440
current day canard of modern day gold price
discovery”

00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:49.230
But for us, the documentary’s cardinal sin
is that it’s boring.

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:53.369
A documentary on gold could be interesting,
should be interesting.

00:36:53.369 --> 00:36:59.119
But this is what you get when filmmakers try
to tell a human story through the filter of

00:36:59.119 --> 00:37:00.200
industry.

00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:04.420
The potential is there, but the documentary
is tethered to corporate interests from which

00:37:04.420 --> 00:37:06.310
it cannot stray.

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:11.360
Gold is so culturally significant in India
that it’s a pillar of the global gold industry.

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:15.800
That’s fascinating, but if you really dug
into it you’d need to talk about the whole

00:37:15.800 --> 00:37:21.400
dowry thing, and your American investors might
find that a bit uncomfortable - best just

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:25.280
not say anything, just talk about how pretty
it is.

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:29.410
If we praise Reefton’s water quality, then
potential investors might learn about the

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:32.460
toxic pit lakes that we normally leave behind

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:34.569
“Wait, what toxic pit problem?”

00:37:34.569 --> 00:37:37.940
So better to just focus on the trees.

00:37:37.940 --> 00:37:42.050
Corporate interests smother the compelling
material, grinding it down to its shallowest

00:37:42.050 --> 00:37:43.050
form.

00:37:43.050 --> 00:37:48.119
Essential material is absent, and what material
is there is inexplicably boring.

00:37:48.119 --> 00:37:53.390
Funny thing is that this isn’t even the
WGC’s first crack at this particular concept.

00:37:53.390 --> 00:37:57.109
In 2021 they released a miniseries called
The Golden Thread.

00:37:57.109 --> 00:38:03.790
Wait, sorry, not a miniseries, a “landmark
immersive documentary series showcasing gold’s

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:07.440
impact on humanity’s past, present and future.”

00:38:07.440 --> 00:38:11.950
The hook for this one was that they found
experts in fields that use gold in some form

00:38:11.950 --> 00:38:16.770
or another, aerospace and the like, and then
have them talk about their projects.

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:23.310
“Gold is critically important to make MOXY
work for a number of reasons”

00:38:23.310 --> 00:38:28.460
But it’s all the same problem, there are
specific interests being served, you can’t

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:33.850
just tell a story where gold is a thematic
connector between all these other interesting

00:38:33.850 --> 00:38:37.710
subjects, you need gold to be the main character.

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:42.820
So the whole thing turns into a parade of
scientists and engineers talking about their

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:48.829
projects as though the most important thing
about it is that gold is involved.

00:38:48.829 --> 00:38:54.470
Like, sure, we detected gravitational waves,
that’s cool and all, but let’s talk about

00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:58.390
how colliding neutron stars generate a lot
of value.

00:38:58.390 --> 00:39:02.810
“What scientists saw that day was gold being
created in space!

00:39:02.810 --> 00:39:08.110
The gold created in this collision will cycle
through the cosmos and perhaps one day will

00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:10.290
form part of another planet”

00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:15.050
The impression isn’t that gold is a kinda
unique material with useful and interesting

00:39:15.050 --> 00:39:17.869
physical properties, but literally magic.

00:39:17.869 --> 00:39:23.290
And when I say “there are interests being
served” and all that, I’m not extrapolating,

00:39:23.290 --> 00:39:25.340
these people are very explicit.

00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:26.930
It’s in the press release!

00:39:26.930 --> 00:39:31.800
“The purpose of the documentary is to increase
the relevance and consideration of gold in

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:34.440
the minds of investors and consumers alike.”

00:39:34.440 --> 00:39:39.180
It’s kinda maddening, I get so used to dealing
with covert propagandists who are trying to

00:39:39.180 --> 00:39:44.940
smuggle in some ulterior agenda and the World
Gold Council just does my job for me.

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:47.090
“Guard it with gold.

00:39:47.090 --> 00:39:53.860
So when the unexpected happens and the world
is in turmoil, you’re protected.”

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:58.290
So, the thing that I find compelling here
is the existence of these corporate propaganda

00:39:58.290 --> 00:40:02.420
pieces, because the fact that someone decided
this was a good project to make, that this

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:07.880
was worth the hundreds of thousands of dollars
that it would have cost even outside of whatever

00:40:07.880 --> 00:40:12.510
Idris Elba was paid, that in and of itself
is an indicator.

00:40:12.510 --> 00:40:17.069
The World Gold Council doesn’t just decide
to hire a celebrity spokesperson and plaster

00:40:17.069 --> 00:40:19.690
his face across the internet because they’re
bored.

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:24.490
They don’t make a documentary about how
cool gold is because they’re just, like,

00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:26.079
they think it’s neat.

00:40:26.079 --> 00:40:30.670
They do it because there’s something that
they think they need to convince us of.

00:40:30.670 --> 00:40:35.390
It ends up so weird and stilted because they’re
trying to talk about narrative and meaning

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:39.050
but those ideas are completely foreign to
the people who produced this.

00:40:39.050 --> 00:40:43.900
I want to go back to that interview with Joe
Cavatoni because it really helps illustrate

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:47.170
that these guys are just living in an alternate
universe.

00:40:47.170 --> 00:40:51.910
So, there’s this phrase we’re all familiar
with at this point: selling shovels during

00:40:51.910 --> 00:40:56.880
a gold rush, the best way to get rich in a
gold rush isn’t to dig for gold, it’s

00:40:56.880 --> 00:40:57.880
to sell shovels.

00:40:57.880 --> 00:41:00.430
It’s kinda a dark phrase, isn’t it?

00:41:00.430 --> 00:41:04.670
Like, at a basic level it speaks to some grimmer
human instincts: opportunism, exploitation,

00:41:04.670 --> 00:41:05.670
delusion.

00:41:05.670 --> 00:41:12.730
It’s a phrase about crass pragmatism in
the face of irrationality.

00:41:12.730 --> 00:41:17.040
You would think that a guy who represents
the interests of the guys buying shovels would

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:22.060
maybe find the phrase a bit distasteful, but
these are capitalists, in the purest sense

00:41:22.060 --> 00:41:23.060
of the word.

00:41:23.060 --> 00:41:27.390
“You know a lot of people fail to remember
that the California gold rush produced a very

00:41:27.390 --> 00:41:30.000
successful and very wealthy man, and that
was Levi Strauss.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:31.829
There’s a bit in the story about it”

00:41:31.829 --> 00:41:33.730
“No, that was a great part of this movie.”

00:41:33.730 --> 00:41:39.109
“Yeah it’s a fun story to understand and
you see how it’s not just about gold and

00:41:39.109 --> 00:41:42.760
mining, but it’s about everything in the
ecosystem around it that brings it to life.”

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:46.370
“Yeah, it’s not just about selling shovels,
it’s about selling jeans, for example, to

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:49.329
those that are using the picks and the axes”

00:41:49.329 --> 00:41:52.349
There’s no sentimentality here.

00:41:52.349 --> 00:41:54.109
There’s money to be made.

00:41:54.109 --> 00:41:57.880
And the thing is that Joe is ultimately a
kind of shovel salesman himself.

00:41:57.880 --> 00:42:03.990
Like, okay, on one hand Joe represents companies
who dig up gold, companies who literally buy

00:42:03.990 --> 00:42:04.990
shovels.

00:42:04.990 --> 00:42:10.180
But the people digging up literal gold aren’t
the metaphorical gold rush here, mines aren’t

00:42:10.180 --> 00:42:12.000
panic-buying machine parts.

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:17.470
The entire thesis of the World Gold Council
is that gold, the asset, is a safe harbour

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:19.050
in a turbulent market.

00:42:19.050 --> 00:42:23.350
It’s not just a thing for cranks and weirdos
with five years of canned beans in their booby

00:42:23.350 --> 00:42:27.920
trapped shed, gold is for slick, elegant,
savvy people.

00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:29.750
Ordinary, yet classy.

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:34.150
The WGC wants you to know that this could
be you.

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:36.160
“Gold is everyone’s asset.”

00:42:36.160 --> 00:42:40.829
We live in turbulent times, and the market
has been socially elevated as the one real

00:42:40.829 --> 00:42:42.569
path for building wealth.

00:42:42.569 --> 00:42:48.339
That’s the gold rush, and Joe, with his
slick looking celebrity documentary and calming

00:42:48.339 --> 00:42:58.260
social media ads is trying to sell you a shovel.

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:01.740
[Dubstep]

00:43:01.740 --> 00:43:36.539
[Idris Elba auto-tuned repeating “Gold”
over and over and over]

